#: Game.Levels.VectorSpaceWorld.Level01
msgid "## Vector Space Definition\n"
"\n"
"We define a vector space §0 over a field §1 as an abelian group with four key axioms.\n"
"In this game, §2 is built on Mathlib's §3 over a field, which already contains\n"
"all the vector space properties:\n"
"\n"
"§4\n"
"\n"
"The four fundamental vector space axioms are:\n"
"- **Distributivity over vector addition:** §5\n"
"- **Distributivity over scalar addition:** §6\n"
"- **Associativity:** §7\n"
"- **Identity:** §8\n"
"\n"
"This educational approach lets us use standard mathematical terminology while leveraging Mathlib's robust\n"
"§9, §10, and §11 definitions, which provide notation such as §12 and §13, and include\n"
"many helpful theorems that we will not need to prove ourselves.\n"
"\n"
"Note that to write the §14 character, type\n"
"\"\\\\smul\".\n"
"\n"
"Also, note that there is some strange §15 text in your objects. This simply means that your objects\n"
"are instances of certain classes, for example that K V is a vector space.\n"
"\n"
"## Goal for this level\n"
"\n"
"In this level, you'll prove that multiplying a vector by the zero scalar (§16) results in the\n"
"zero vector (§17). This is going to be a slightly involved proof, so it is important to get an\n"
"understanding of the proof before beginning to write it.\n"
"\n"
"The first step of a normal proof would be writing §18 as §19, then using distributivity\n"
"to get it as §20. Lastly, cancelling out a §21 on each side gets §22.\n"
"\n"
"However, this proof relies on our assumptions and constructs the goal from them. This can be done in\n"
"Lean, however, it requires using the §23 tactic, and is unnecessarily complex. In Lean, proofs are\n"
"often done backwards, working from the goal and creating the hypotheses.\n"
"\n"
"Doing the proof backwards thus must first involve adding §24 to both sides of the goal, undoing\n"
"distributivity, then cancelling out some zeros.\n"
"\n"
"### add_right_cancel\n"
"\n"
"To use cancellation of addition, we need a new theorem, §25. This theorem is a proof\n"
"that §26. Since your goal is of the form §27, §28 will\n"
"change the goal to §29. However, you want to be able to write the value of §30, but\n"
"in this case, Lean doesn't know what value you want to add. You can tell Lean what to add to the\n"
"equation by §31, replacing the question marks with whatever you\n"
"want to add.\n"
"\n"
"### zero_add\n"
"\n"
"We also need to know some basic theorems about addition. For both the scalars and vectors, adding any\n"
"vector to §32 will result in that vector. Simply §33 will show this.\n"
"\n"
"### symm\n"
"\n"
"You will also need the §34 tactic. §35 stands for symmetry of equality, and it can be used to\n"
"change goals of the form §36 to §37. §38 will also change a hypothesis §39 to\n"
"§40. However, neither of these uses are needed in this level. You may\n"
"notice that if §41 is a hypothesis, rw[h] will rewrite all §42s to §43s. What if you want to\n"
"rewrite all §44s to §45s, you can instead do §46.\n"
"\n"
"Also note that when the theorem has a §47 symbol, as in §48, you need to\n"
"specify what §49 and §50 you mean to use before using §51. For example, you would have to write\n"
"§52.\n"
"\n"
"One last hint is that when writing §53, you often want to specify which zero you are talking about.\n"
"We know that the natural numbers, for example, has a §54, but so do the Field K and the Abelian Group\n"
"V, and those §55s are different. To specify which §56 you are talking about, write §57 or §58.\n"
"\n"
"### Note on simp and linarith\n"
"\n"
"In this world, we are primarily proving simple statements about vector spaces. This is exactly what\n"
"the §59 and §60 tactics are meant to do. In fact, the §61 tactic alone would be able to\n"
"solve the first three levels of this world. Because of this, you will not be able to use those tactics\n"
"in this world.\n"
"\n"
"**Note:** If you see hints appearing multiple times, this is a known issue with the game framework. Simply continue with your proof - the level will work correctly despite any duplicate hints."
msgstr ""

