#: Game.Levels.LinearIndependenceSpanWorld.Level09
msgid "This is the second challenge level, and the last level of the Linear Independence and Span World! Similar\n"
"to the first challenge level, this level is optional and you can play through it if you want more practice.\n"
"If you find it too difficult, you can skip to the next world and come back later, or enable \"relaxed\" rules\n"
"in the game settings.\n"
"\n"
"### The Goal\n"
"The goal of this level is to prove that if you have some set §0, and some vector §1 inside the span\n"
"of §2, the span of §3 is the same as the span of §4. This is because §5 can be written\n"
"as a sum of vectors of §6, so any time you have §7 appear in a linear combination of §8, you\n"
"can simply replace it with a sum of vectors in §9.\n"
"\n"
"**Note:** This level may experience a hint display issue where hints repeat. If you see the same hint multiple times, the level is still working correctly - just continue with your proof as normal.\n"
"\n"
"### Proof overview\n"
"The most difficult part of this proof is showing that given a linear representation of a vector in the\n"
"span of §10, we can represent it as a sum of vectors in §11. You are able to represent a sum over\n"
"§12 as a sum over §13 plus the function applied to §14. Then, rewrite §15 as a sum of vectors\n"
"in §16, and recombine the sums."
msgstr ""

